We conducted a brief online cross-sectional survey in English and Spanish that is used to inform a network-based model of a hypothetical place-based intervention. Data were collected in New York City between August and November 2022. Potential participants were included in the study if they consented to participate, were over 18 years of age, identified as transgender, gay, bisexual, or as men who have sex with other men, and were in New York City in the four weeks preceding their participation. Potential participants were excluded if they were younger than 18 years of age or they withdrew consent.

### Person-place question {.unnumbered}

ITs not enough to know home address, but it is hard to do research because we cant ask about places easily. Names are sometimes sensitive or impossible (in the case of residences), Zip codes are difficult to remember and addresses are worse. We designed, tested, and implemented a novel online survey instrument, including a newly designed question format.[^longnote]

To collect anonymous information on sensitive locations (such as sex parties) by prompting the participant to indicate places of a specified type on a representation of the map of New York City. We use the Google maps interface - the location-input question displays a map with detailed geographic information including place names, roads, and neighborhood names, and includes a search bar (see @fig-mapinputformat). In addition to these, it is searchable by location name, approximate address, cross streets, neighborhood name.


